|---------|\n| this \u2192 other | Business Intent | many | It is built from a business intent |\n| this \u2192 other | Capability | one | It belongs to a capability \u2026 |\n| this \u2192 other | Functionality | one | \u2026 or to a functionality |\n| other \u2192 this | Work | many | A piece of work carries it \u2014 the intent its agent works on |\n\n**Rules**\n\n- It is workable only when it is built from at least one confirmed business intent (or one since met). An ICE with no business intent, or only proposed ones, is kept as written but no play plans, breaks down or builds from it; linking it to a confirmed intent makes it workable.\n- Writing it is never blocked: any play may write or add to it, inside that play's declared write scope, keeping what is there.\n- It is written at the depth that is known: a light ICE is goals only; a full one has every label.\n- Once aligned, one part of the product has one ICE; two ICE for the same part are drift for alignment to merge.\n\n**Example**\n\n> The last section of `orders/checkout/guest-checkout/functionality.md`, whose spine entry has `intents: [guest-checkout]`:\n>\n> ## Agent intent (ICE)\n> - Goal: A shopper with no account completes a purchase from start to finish.\n> - Limit: Nothing about how returns work changes for any shopper.\n> - Fails if: a guest shopper is asked to sign up before paying.\n> - Context: a guest shopper on mobile, the payment service and the order store; card and wallet are in, saved addresses are out.\n> - Checked by: an end-to-end test in which a guest completes checkout in under two minutes.\n>\n> A light ICE: the last section of `capabilities/harness-steering/capability.md`, written by `/intent` from a prototype, in the holding folder because no domain was known yet. Its spine entry's `intents` names \"Know whether the harnesses we build are useful and drive agent autonomy\" (confirmed \u2014 so it is workable):\n>\n> ## Agent intent (ICE)\n> - Goal: For each harness, tell how often an agent finishes its work without a person stepping in.\n\n## Not yet defined\n\n- **Work** \u2014 a piece of work agents do: a Feature, Story, Chore, Bug or Spike; it serves a business intent and carries the ICE its agent works on.\n- **Tracker Issue** \u2014 an issue in the team's tracker (GitHub, Jira, \u2026) that tracks a business intent or a piece of work.\n- **Domain** \u2014 a big area of the product (exists today in `product-os.yaml`). Its page is light (directional) or full (detailed); it holds no ICE; its spine `intents` names the business intents that touch it.\n- **Capability** \u2014 something the product can do, inside a domain (exists today in `product-os.yaml`). Its page is directional or detailed, and carries its ICE in the `## Agent intent (ICE)` section. It may sit in the holding folder `capabilities/<id>/` until its domain is known.\n- **Functionality** \u2014 one specific action inside a capability (the leaf of `product-os.yaml`). Its page carries its ICE in the `## Agent intent (ICE)` section.\n\n## Log\n\n- 2026-10-08 \u2014 First version, built through `build-product-ontology` for #612: six questions; kinds Business Intent, Source and ICE (reused from `ice.yaml`); Work, Tracker Issue, Domain, Capability and Function named but not yet defined. Working notes: `.garura/project/issues/612/specs/ontology-run.md`.\n- 2026-10-08 \u2014 v2: a Source is anything that explains the intent \u2014 a prototype, a document, or a plain statement \u2014 not only a prototype (#612, approving `/intent`'s intent). Snapshot and Kind widened to match.\n- 2026-10-08 \u2014 A Source may be a project folder (its written docs are kept; the running app is captured as a second, site Source).\n- 2026-10-09 \u2014 v3 (#612): no play owns a kind \u2014 any play writes what it finds, in any order; rules say what holds once aligned and never block a write; drift goes to the Alignment drive. Only a person confirms or drops a business intent. The writer rule for ICE is gone. (Where ICE lives and when it is workable: see the next entry, which replaced a first idea of separate ICE files.)\n- 2026-10-09 \u2014 Review of #631: \"Function\" renamed \"Functionality\" to match the glossary and the spine; any play may write a kind only inside its own declared write scope (`direct-model-write.md`).\n- 2026-10-09 \u2014 On review of #631: ICE lives inline in its node's grounding doc (spine v2), never as a separate file; a capability may exist before its domain (`/vision` attaches it); an ICE is workable only when built from a confirmed business intent (spine `intents`) \u2014 unlinked ICE is kept, not worked on, never blocked.\n- 2026-10-17 \u2014 v4 (#637): one product model that every play writes, at the depth it knows, and nothing waits for another play. ICE now has one named place \u2014 `## Agent intent (ICE)`, the last section of a capability's or functionality's page, in labelled lines filled as far as is known; expected once the node names a business intent (missing is a linter warning, so earlier pages still pass). A business intent's touches are kept on its page as a **Touches** list (adds / changes / removes, with links), and the domain's spine entry gains `intents`, so every part an intent touches is linked both ways. A domain page is light (directional) or full (detailed), like a capability's. Design: `.garura/project/issues/637/specs/design.md`.\n- 2026-10-18 \u2014 #656: personal names taken out of the Source example and of this Log, so the ontology can live in Phoenix memory for any project. No kind, field or rule changed."}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-002", "issue": 1, "play": "intent", "question": "Which kinds and rules does this draft follow (second pass, after the answers)?", "answer": "The Business Intent kind: title, outcome, why, asked by and proof are required, must not optional, each filled only from a source or the person, and only a person confirms or drops it. The ICE kind: goals first, built from a business intent, its capability may wait for a domain.", "decided_by": "business-intent-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:27:10Z", "trace": [{"asked": "the Business Intent and ICE kinds, their properties and rules", "found": "Business Intent properties, rules and states; Source; ICE properties and rules", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "standards/schemas/product-os/ontology.md", "start": 1, "end": 206, "fingerprint": "sha256:c07cbc674d46f6748637293ad96c5ad6b7841e98b02caa95464ce74639523f3c", "text": "---\nontology: product\nversion: 4\nupdated: 2026-10-18\n---\n\n# Product Ontology\n\nThe agreed map of what a product is made of in Garura: the kinds of things, what each holds, how they connect, and what must always be true. A person and an agent reading it use the same words for the same things. It grows one kind at a time, through the `build-product-ontology` skill; this first version defines the Business Intent \u2014 the person's side \u2014 and connects it to ICE, the agent's side.\n\nHow it flows: a person gives an intent \u2192 it becomes a **Business Intent** here and a \"Business Intent\" issue in the tracker \u2192 plays break it into **Work** agents know (Feature, Story, Chore, Bug, Spike), each carrying the **ICE** its agent works on \u2192 as work finishes, the Business Intent is updated with what was done \u2192 when it is done, the Business Intent links to the value it got through the tracker's finished issues.\n\n**Who writes, and what the rules mean.** No play owns a kind. Any play may write the intents, ICE or other kinds it finds \u2014 from a prototype, while shaping, or in the middle of implementation \u2014 and the parts may come in any order. A play still writes only inside its own declared write scope (`standards/rules/direct-model-write.md`); a play's scope is widened on purpose, in that play, never by this sentence. The rules below say what holds once the product is **aligned**; they never stop a write. Drift between them is found and fixed by the Alignment drive. What a rule can stop is work, never a write: an ICE with no confirmed business intent is kept, but nothing is built from it. One thing is never left to alignment: only a person confirms or drops a business intent.\n\n## Questions it must answer\n\n- **Q1** \u2014 Given a business intent and the product as it is today, which types of work does it need \u2014 Feature, Story, Bug, Chore, Spike \u2014 what value does each bring to the intent, and when would a type of work be wrong for it?\n  **Answered by:** Business Intent; Work serves Business Intent; Business Intent touches Capability\n  **Status:** partly\n- **Q2** \u2014 Given a new business intent, which parts of the product does it touch \u2014 and for each, does it change something that exists, add something new, or remove something \u2014 at the level of a domain, a capability or a functionality?\n  **Answered by:** Business Intent touches Domain, Capability, Functionality (each marked change, add or remove)\n  **Status:** partly\n- **Q3** \u2014 When two intents touch the same part of the product and want opposite things, is that shown to a person before any work starts?\n  **Answered by:** Business Intent touches Capability, compared across intents\n  **Status:** partly\n- **Q4** \u2014 For this intent, which work did agents finish on their own, and where did a person have to step in?\n  **Answered by:** Work serves Business Intent\n  **Status:** not yet\n- **Q5** \u2014 What was this intent drawn from (its source), and what did it show at the time?\n  **Answered by:** Source shows Business Intent\n  **Status:** answered\n- **Q6** \u2014 Is this intent met yet, and how would we know?\n  **Answered by:** Business Intent (its proof and its stage)\n  **Status:** answered\n\n## Kinds\n\n### Business Intent\n\n**What it is:** What a person or the organisation wants \u2014 an outcome, in their words, with why it matters and how we will know it is met. It is the person's interface to the product: the destination that all work and every ICE points at. It is the same thing as the \"Business Intent\" issue type in the tracker. It is not ICE (the agent's intent) and not a piece of work.\n**Who reads it:** person\n**Lives at:** `product-os/intents/<id>.md` \u2014 one plain-words page per business intent\n\n**Properties**\n\n| Property | What it holds | Required |\n|----------|---------------|----------|\n| Title | The intent in one line, in the person's words | yes |\n| Outcome | What should be true when it is met | yes |\n| Why | Why it matters to the business | yes |\n| Asked by | The person who wants it | yes |\n| Proof it is met | How we will know the outcome is true | yes |\n| Must not | Limits the person stated | no |\n| Stage | proposed, confirmed, met, or dropped | yes |\n| Confirmed by and when | Who agreed it says the right thing, and when | yes, once confirmed |\n| Touches | Every domain, capability and functionality it reaches into, each marked adds, changes or removes, with a link to that part's page \u2014 the **Touches** list on its page, written by a script whenever a play links a part to it | no, until a part is linked |\n| What was done | What was delivered for it and the value each piece brought, with links to the finished issues \u2014 updated as work finishes | no, until work finishes |\n\n**Relationships**\n\n| Direction | Other kind | Count | Meaning |\n|-----------|-----------|-------|---------|\n| other \u2192 this | Source | many | A source shows what is wanted |\n| other \u2192 this | ICE | many | An ICE is built from it \u2014 the agent's intent for one part of the product |\n| other \u2192 this | Work | many | A piece of work serves it |\n| this \u2192 other | Tracker Issue | one | It is tracked as one \"Business Intent\" issue in the team's tracker |\n| this \u2192 other | Domain | many | It touches a domain \u2014 marked change, add or remove (its Touches list; the domain's spine `intents` holds the other side) |\n| this \u2192 other | Capability | many | It touches a capability \u2014 marked change, add or remove (its Touches list; the capability's spine `intents` holds the other side) |\n| this \u2192 other | Functionality | many | It touches a functionality \u2014 marked change, add or remove (its Touches list; the functionality's spine `intents` holds the other side) |\n\n**Rules**\n\n- It is a destination: sources, ICE and work point at it; it points only at the tracker issue it is tracked as and the parts of the product it touches.\n- Each part it touches is linked both ways: the part's spine entry names it in `intents`, and its own Touches list names the part with the mark. A play that makes one side writes the other.\n- It is met by its proof, never because its work is finished.\n- Work starts only from an ICE built from a confirmed business intent. An ICE or a piece of work found before its intent is confirmed \u2014 or before it exists \u2014 is kept, not worked on, and is drift for alignment, never a blocked write.\n- A source is never copied into it; the source shows the intent, it is not the intent.\n- It is written for a business reader, in plain words.\n- Only a person confirms or drops it; an agent never does.\n- Two intents that touch the same part of the product in opposite ways are a clash, shown to a person before work starts.\n\n**States**\n\nproposed \u2192 confirmed (the person who asked confirms it says the right thing) \u2192 met (its proof holds). A proposed or confirmed intent can be dropped (a person drops it, with a reason).\n\n**Example**\n\n> **Shoppers can check out without making an account**\n>\n> **Outcome:** A shopper with no account can buy, start to finish.\n> **Why:** 4 in 10 shoppers leave at the sign-up step.\n> **Asked by:** Priya, Head of Growth\n> **Proof it is met:** Fewer than 2 in 10 shoppers leave at sign-up, for a month.\n> **Must not:** Change how returns work.\n> **Stage:** confirmed \u2014 by Priya, 8 October 2026\n> **Source:** the checkout prototype, read 8 October 2026\n> **Touches:**\n> - [Orders](../orders/domain.md) (domain) \u2014 changes\n> - [Checkout](../orders/checkout/capability.md) (capability) \u2014 changes\n> - [Guest checkout](../orders/checkout/guest-checkout/functionality.md) (functionality) \u2014 adds\n> **What was done:** (nothing yet)\n\n### Source\n\n**What it is:** What a business intent was drawn from \u2014 anything that explains it: a prototype (a single file, a project folder, or a deployed site), a document, or a plain statement \u2014 kept exactly as it was when read. It shows the intent; it is never the intent. Named \"Source\" because Garura's \"Evidence\" already means a play's run record.\n**Who reads it:** both\n**Lives at:** saved through Garura's evidence method at the reading play's close: `{product_base}_evidence/intent/<timestamp>/` \u2014 the snapshot plus a `source.md` describing it\n\n**Properties**\n\n| Property | What it holds | Required |\n|----------|---------------|----------|\n| Kind | What sort of source \u2014 \"prototype \u2014 deployed site\", \"prototype \u2014 file\", \"prototype \u2014 project\", \"document\", or \"statement\" | yes |\n| Read on | When it was read | yes |\n| Snapshot | Everything needed to see it again, kept even if the link or file goes away: screenshots and images for a site, a copy of the file for a file or a document, the exact text for a statement | yes |\n| What it shows | A plain summary of what a person sees and can do in it | yes |\n| Given by | The git user who ran the reading play | yes |\n\n**Relationships**\n\n| Direction | Other kind | Count | Meaning |\n|-----------|-----------|-------|---------|\n| this \u2192 other | Business Intent | many | It shows what a business intent wants |\n\n**Rules**\n\n- It is kept as it was when read; the snapshot never changes afterwards.\n- It is never copied into a business intent.\n- One source can show more than one business intent, and one business intent can have more than one source.\n\n**Example**\n\n> **Source \u2014 checkout prototype**\n> **Kind:** prototype \u2014 deployed site\n> **Read on:** 8 October 2026\n> **Snapshot:** `_evidence/intent/20261008-1412/` \u2014 6 screenshots, from the cart to the order confirmation\n> **What it shows:** A checkout with a \"continue as guest\" button; no sign-up step; card and wallet payments.\n> **Given by:** alex-example\n\n### ICE\n\n**What it is:** The agent's intent for one part of the product \u2014 a capability or a functionality: what must be true there, for whom, and how it will be checked. It is what an agent works on, and it is built from a business intent. It starts with the goals a business intent needs; context, outcomes and quality needs are added as the product is shaped. Its capability may exist before that capability has a domain \u2014 `/vision` attaches the domain later.\n**Who reads it:** agent\n**Lives at:** one named section, `## Agent intent (ICE)`, always the last section of its capability's or functionality's grounding doc (`grounding/capability.md`, `grounding/functionality.md`) \u2014 labelled lines (Goal, Limit, Fails if, Context, Checked by, Quality need) filled as far as is known; the spine entry's `intents` names the business intents it is built from. The section is expected once that link exists; a linked node without it is a warning for alignment, never a stop, so pages written before v4 still pass.\n\n**Properties**\n\n| Property | What it holds | Required |\n|----------|---------------|----------|\n| Part of the product | The capability or functionality it belongs to | yes |\n| Goals, limits, failures | What must be true, the limits, and what counts as failing | goals yes; limits and failures once aligned |\n| Context | Who it serves (personas), the systems it touches, what is in and out of scope | once aligned |\n| Outcomes | How each goal is checked | once aligned |\n| Quality and compliance needs | Concrete targets (speed, security, \u2026) and regimes such as PCI | no |\n| Built from | The business intent(s) it serves \u2014 its node's spine `intents` | to be workable |\n\n**Relationships**\n\n| Direction | Other kind | Count | Meaning |\n|-----------|-----------|-------|---------|\n| this \u2192 other | Business Intent | many | It is built from a business intent |\n| this \u2192 other | Capability | one | It belongs to a capability \u2026 |\n| this \u2192 other | Functionality | one | \u2026 or to a functionality |\n| other \u2192 this | Work | many | A piece of work carries it \u2014 the intent its agent works on |\n\n**Rules**\n\n- It is workable only when it is built from at least one confirmed business intent (or one since met). An ICE with no business intent, or only proposed ones, is kept as written but no play plans, breaks down or builds from it; linking it to a confirmed intent makes it workable.\n- Writing it is never blocked: any play may write or add to it, inside that play's declared write scope, keeping what is there.\n- It is written at the depth that is known: a light ICE is goals only; a full one has every label.\n- Once aligned, one part of the product has one ICE; two ICE for the same part are drift for alignment to merge.\n\n**Example**\n\n> The last section of `orders/checkout/guest-checkout/functionality.md`, whose spine entry has `intents: [guest-checkout]`:\n>\n> ## Agent intent (ICE)\n> - Goal: A shopper with no account completes a purchase from start to finish.\n> - Limit: Nothing about how returns work changes for any shopper.\n> - Fails if: a guest shopper is asked to sign up before paying.\n> - Context: a guest shopper on mobile, the payment service and the order store; card and wallet are in, saved addresses are out.\n> - Checked by: an end-to-end test in which a guest completes checkout in under two minutes.\n>\n> A light ICE: the last section of `capabilities/harness-steering/capability.md`, written by `/intent` from a prototype, in the holding folder because no domain was known yet. Its spine entry's `intents` names \"Know whether the harnesses we build are useful and drive agent autonomy\" (confirmed \u2014 so it is workable):\n>\n> ## Agent intent (ICE)\n> - Goal: For each harness, tell how often an agent finishes its work without a person stepping in.\n\n## Not yet defined\n\n- **Work** \u2014 a piece of work agents do: a Feature, Story, Chore, Bug or Spike; it serves a business intent and carries the ICE its agent works on.\n- **Tracker Issue** \u2014 an issue in the team's tracker (GitHub, Jira, \u2026) that tracks a business intent or a piece of work.\n- **Domain** \u2014 a big area of the product (exists today in `product-os.yaml`). Its page is light (directional) or full (detailed); it holds no ICE; its spine `intents` names the business intents that touch it.\n- **Capability** \u2014 something the product can do, inside a domain (exists today in `product-os.yaml`). Its page is directional or detailed, and carries its ICE in the `## Agent intent (ICE)` section. It may sit in the holding folder `capabilities/<id>/` until its domain is known.\n- **Functionality** \u2014 one specific action inside a capability (the leaf of `product-os.yaml`). Its page carries its ICE in the `## Agent intent (ICE)` section.\n\n## Log\n\n- 2026-10-08 \u2014 First version, built through `build-product-ontology` for #612: six questions; kinds Business Intent, Source and ICE (reused from `ice.yaml`); Work, Tracker Issue, Domain, Capability and Function named but not yet defined. Working notes: `.garura/project/issues/612/specs/ontology-run.md`.\n- 2026-10-08 \u2014 v2: a Source is anything that explains the intent \u2014 a prototype, a document, or a plain statement \u2014 not only a prototype (#612, approving `/intent`'s intent). Snapshot and Kind widened to match.\n- 2026-10-08 \u2014 A Source may be a project folder (its written docs are kept; the running app is captured as a second, site Source).\n- 2026-10-09 \u2014 v3 (#612): no play owns a kind \u2014 any play writes what it finds, in any order; rules say what holds once aligned and never block a write; drift goes to the Alignment drive. Only a person confirms or drops a business intent. The writer rule for ICE is gone. (Where ICE lives and when it is workable: see the next entry, which replaced a first idea of separate ICE files.)\n- 2026-10-09 \u2014 Review of #631: \"Function\" renamed \"Functionality\" to match the glossary and the spine; any play may write a kind only inside its own declared write scope (`direct-model-write.md`).\n- 2026-10-09 \u2014 On review of #631: ICE lives inline in its node's grounding doc (spine v2), never as a separate file; a capability may exist before its domain (`/vision` attaches it); an ICE is workable only when built from a confirmed business intent (spine `intents`) \u2014 unlinked ICE is kept, not worked on, never blocked.\n- 2026-10-17 \u2014 v4 (#637): one product model that every play writes, at the depth it knows, and nothing waits for another play. ICE now has one named place \u2014 `## Agent intent (ICE)`, the last section of a capability's or functionality's page, in labelled lines filled as far as is known; expected once the node names a business intent (missing is a linter warning, so earlier pages still pass). A business intent's touches are kept on its page as a **Touches** list (adds / changes / removes, with links), and the domain's spine entry gains `intents`, so every part an intent touches is linked both ways. A domain page is light (directional) or full (detailed), like a capability's. Design: `.garura/project/issues/637/specs/design.md`.\n- 2026-10-18 \u2014 #656: personal names taken out of the Source example and of this Log, so the ontology can live in Phoenix memory for any project. No kind, field or rule changed.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 1, "end": 206, "text": "---\nontology: product\nversion: 4\nupdated: 2026-10-18\n---\n\n# Product Ontology\n\nThe agreed map of what a product is made of in Garura: the kinds of things, what each holds, how they connect, and what must always be true. A person and an agent reading it use the same words for the same things. It grows one kind at a time, through the `build-product-ontology` skill; this first version defines the Business Intent \u2014 the person's side \u2014 and connects it to ICE, the agent's side.\n\nHow it flows: a person gives an intent \u2192 it becomes a **Business Intent** here and a \"Business Intent\" issue in the tracker \u2192 plays break it into **Work** agents know (Feature, Story, Chore, Bug, Spike), each carrying the **ICE** its agent works on \u2192 as work finishes, the Business Intent is updated with what was done \u2192 when it is done, the Business Intent links to the value it got through the tracker's finished issues.\n\n**Who writes, and what the rules mean.** No play owns a kind. Any play may write the intents, ICE or other kinds it finds \u2014 from a prototype, while shaping, or in the middle of implementation \u2014 and the parts may come in any order. A play still writes only inside its own declared write scope (`standards/rules/direct-model-write.md`); a play's scope is widened on purpose, in that play, never by this sentence. The rules below say what holds once the product is **aligned**; they never stop a write. Drift between them is found and fixed by the Alignment drive. What a rule can stop is work, never a write: an ICE with no confirmed business intent is kept, but nothing is built from it. One thing is never left to alignment: only a person confirms or drops a business intent.\n\n## Questions it must answer\n\n- **Q1** \u2014 Given a business intent and the product as it is today, which types of work does it need \u2014 Feature, Story, Bug, Chore, Spike \u2014 what value does each bring to the intent, and when would a type of work be wrong for it?\n  **Answered by:** Business Intent; Work serves Business Intent; Business Intent touches Capability\n  **Status:** partly\n- **Q2** \u2014 Given a new business intent, which parts of the product does it touch \u2014 and for each, does it change something that exists, add something new, or remove something \u2014 at the level of a domain, a capability or a functionality?\n  **Answered by:** Business Intent touches Domain, Capability, Functionality (each marked change, add or remove)\n  **Status:** partly\n- **Q3** \u2014 When two intents touch the same part of the product and want opposite things, is that shown to a person before any work starts?\n  **Answered by:** Business Intent touches Capability, compared across intents\n  **Status:** partly\n- **Q4** \u2014 For this intent, which work did agents finish on their own, and where did a person have to step in?\n  **Answered by:** Work serves Business Intent\n  **Status:** not yet\n- **Q5** \u2014 What was this intent drawn from (its source), and what did it show at the time?\n  **Answered by:** Source shows Business Intent\n  **Status:** answered\n- **Q6** \u2014 Is this intent met yet, and how would we know?\n  **Answered by:** Business Intent (its proof and its stage)\n  **Status:** answered\n\n## Kinds\n\n### Business Intent\n\n**What it is:** What a person or the organisation wants \u2014 an outcome, in their words, with why it matters and how we will know it is met. It is the person's interface to the product: the destination that all work and every ICE points at. It is the same thing as the \"Business Intent\" issue type in the tracker. It is not ICE (the agent's intent) and not a piece of work.\n**Who reads it:** person\n**Lives at:** `product-os/intents/<id>.md` \u2014 one plain-words page per business intent\n\n**Properties**\n\n| Property | What it holds | Required |\n|----------|---------------|----------|\n| Title | The intent in one line, in the person's words | yes |\n| Outcome | What should be true when it is met | yes |\n| Why | Why it matters to the business | yes |\n| Asked by | The person who wants it | yes |\n| Proof it is met | How we will know the outcome is true | yes |\n| Must not | Limits the person stated | no |\n| Stage | proposed, confirmed, met, or dropped | yes |\n| Confirmed by and when | Who agreed it says the right thing, and when | yes, once confirmed |\n| Touches | Every domain, capability and functionality it reaches into, each marked adds, changes or removes, with a link to that part's page \u2014 the **Touches** list on its page, written by a script whenever a play links a part to it | no, until a part is linked |\n| What was done | What was delivered for it and the value each piece brought, with links to the finished issues \u2014 updated as work finishes | no, until work finishes |\n\n**Relationships**\n\n| Direction | Other kind | Count | Meaning |\n|-----------|-----------|-------|---------|\n| other \u2192 this | Source | many | A source shows what is wanted |\n| other \u2192 this | ICE | many | An ICE is built from it \u2014 the agent's intent for one part of the product |\n| other \u2192 this | Work | many | A piece of work serves it |\n| this \u2192 other | Tracker Issue | one | It is tracked as one \"Business Intent\" issue in the team's tracker |\n| this \u2192 other | Domain | many | It touches a domain \u2014 marked change, add or remove (its Touches list; the domain's spine `intents` holds the other side) |\n| this \u2192 other | Capability | many | It touches a capability \u2014 marked change, add or remove (its Touches list; the capability's spine `intents` holds the other side) |\n| this \u2192 other | Functionality | many | It touches a functionality \u2014 marked change, add or remove (its Touches list; the functionality's spine `intents` holds the other side) |\n\n**Rules**\n\n- It is a destination: sources, ICE and work point at it; it points only at the tracker issue it is tracked as and the parts of the product it touches.\n- Each part it touches is linked both ways: the part's spine entry names it in `intents`, and its own Touches list names the part with the mark. A play that makes one side writes the other.\n- It is met by its proof, never because its work is finished.\n- Work starts only from an ICE built from a confirmed business intent. An ICE or a piece of work found before its intent is confirmed \u2014 or before it exists \u2014 is kept, not worked on, and is drift for alignment, never a blocked write.\n- A source is never copied into it; the source shows the intent, it is not the intent.\n- It is written for a business reader, in plain words.\n- Only a person confirms or drops it; an agent never does.\n- Two intents that touch the same part of the product in opposite ways are a clash, shown to a person before work starts.\n\n**States**\n\nproposed \u2192 confirmed (the person who asked confirms it says the right thing) \u2192 met (its proof holds). A proposed or confirmed intent can be dropped (a person drops it, with a reason).\n\n**Example**\n\n> **Shoppers can check out without making an account**\n>\n> **Outcome:** A shopper with no account can buy, start to finish.\n> **Why:** 4 in 10 shoppers leave at the sign-up step.\n> **Asked by:** Priya, Head of Growth\n> **Proof it is met:** Fewer than 2 in 10 shoppers leave at sign-up, for a month.\n> **Must not:** Change how returns work.\n> **Stage:** confirmed \u2014 by Priya, 8 October 2026\n> **Source:** the checkout prototype, read 8 October 2026\n> **Touches:**\n> - [Orders](../orders/domain.md) (domain) \u2014 changes\n> - [Checkout](../orders/checkout/capability.md) (capability) \u2014 changes\n> - [Guest checkout](../orders/checkout/guest-checkout/functionality.md) (functionality) \u2014 adds\n> **What was done:** (nothing yet)\n\n### Source\n\n**What it is:** What a business intent was drawn from \u2014 anything that explains it: a prototype (a single file, a project folder, or a deployed site), a document, or a plain statement \u2014 kept exactly as it was when read. It shows the intent; it is never the intent. Named \"Source\" because Garura's \"Evidence\" already means a play's run record.\n**Who reads it:** both\n**Lives at:** saved through Garura's evidence method at the reading play's close: `{product_base}_evidence/intent/<timestamp>/` \u2014 the snapshot plus a `source.md` describing it\n\n**Properties**\n\n| Property | What it holds | Required |\n|----------|---------------|----------|\n| Kind | What sort of source \u2014 \"prototype \u2014 deployed site\", \"prototype \u2014 file\", \"prototype \u2014 project\", \"document\", or \"statement\" | yes |\n| Read on | When it was read | yes |\n| Snapshot | Everything needed to see it again, kept even if the link or file goes away: screenshots and images for a site, a copy of the file for a file or a document, the exact text for a statement | yes |\n| What it shows | A plain summary of what a person sees and can do in it | yes |\n| Given by | The git user who ran the reading play | yes |\n\n**Relationships**\n\n| Direction | Other kind | Count | Meaning |\n|-----------|-----------|-------|---------|\n| this \u2192 other | Business Intent | many | It shows what a business intent wants |\n\n**Rules**\n\n- It is kept as it was when read; the snapshot never changes afterwards.\n- It is never copied into a business intent.\n- One source can show more than one business intent, and one business intent can have more than one source.\n\n**Example**\n\n> **Source \u2014 checkout prototype**\n> **Kind:** prototype \u2014 deployed site\n> **Read on:** 8 October 2026\n> **Snapshot:** `_evidence/intent/20261008-1412/` \u2014 6 screenshots, from the cart to the order confirmation\n> **What it shows:** A checkout with a \"continue as guest\" button; no sign-up step; card and wallet payments.\n> **Given by:** alex-example\n\n### ICE\n\n**What it is:** The agent's intent for one part of the product \u2014 a capability or a functionality: what must be true there, for whom, and how it will be checked. It is what an agent works on, and it is built from a business intent. It starts with the goals a business intent needs; context, outcomes and quality needs are added as the product is shaped. Its capability may exist before that capability has a domain \u2014 `/vision` attaches the domain later.\n**Who reads it:** agent\n**Lives at:** one named section, `## Agent intent (ICE)`, always the last section of its capability's or functionality's grounding doc (`grounding/capability.md`, `grounding/functionality.md`) \u2014 labelled lines (Goal, Limit, Fails if, Context, Checked by, Quality need) filled as far as is known; the spine entry's `intents` names the business intents it is built from. The section is expected once that link exists; a linked node without it is a warning for alignment, never a stop, so pages written before v4 still pass.\n\n**Properties**\n\n| Property | What it holds | Required |\n|----------|---------------|----------|\n| Part of the product | The capability or functionality it belongs to | yes |\n| Goals, limits, failures | What must be true, the limits, and what counts as failing | goals yes; limits and failures once aligned |\n| Context | Who it serves (personas), the systems it touches, what is in and out of scope | once aligned |\n| Outcomes | How each goal is checked | once aligned |\n| Quality and compliance needs | Concrete targets (speed, security, \u2026) and regimes such as PCI | no |\n| Built from | The business intent(s) it serves \u2014 its node's spine `intents` | to be workable |\n\n**Relationships**\n\n| Direction | Other kind | Count | Meaning |\n|-----------|-----------|-------|---------|\n| this \u2192 other | Business Intent | many | It is built from a business intent |\n| this \u2192 other | Capability | one | It belongs to a capability \u2026 |\n| this \u2192 other | Functionality | one | \u2026 or to a functionality |\n| other \u2192 this | Work | many | A piece of work carries it \u2014 the intent its agent works on |\n\n**Rules**\n\n- It is workable only when it is built from at least one confirmed business intent (or one since met). An ICE with no business intent, or only proposed ones, is kept as written but no play plans, breaks down or builds from it; linking it to a confirmed intent makes it workable.\n- Writing it is never blocked: any play may write or add to it, inside that play's declared write scope, keeping what is there.\n- It is written at the depth that is known: a light ICE is goals only; a full one has every label.\n- Once aligned, one part of the product has one ICE; two ICE for the same part are drift for alignment to merge.\n\n**Example**\n\n> The last section of `orders/checkout/guest-checkout/functionality.md`, whose spine entry has `intents: [guest-checkout]`:\n>\n> ## Agent intent (ICE)\n> - Goal: A shopper with no account completes a purchase from start to finish.\n> - Limit: Nothing about how returns work changes for any shopper.\n> - Fails if: a guest shopper is asked to sign up before paying.\n> - Context: a guest shopper on mobile, the payment service and the order store; card and wallet are in, saved addresses are out.\n> - Checked by: an end-to-end test in which a guest completes checkout in under two minutes.\n>\n> A light ICE: the last section of `capabilities/harness-steering/capability.md`, written by `/intent` from a prototype, in the holding folder because no domain was known yet. Its spine entry's `intents` names \"Know whether the harnesses we build are useful and drive agent autonomy\" (confirmed \u2014 so it is workable):\n>\n> ## Agent intent (ICE)\n> - Goal: For each harness, tell how often an agent finishes its work without a person stepping in.\n\n## Not yet defined\n\n- **Work** \u2014 a piece of work agents do: a Feature, Story, Chore, Bug or Spike; it serves a business intent and carries the ICE its agent works on.\n- **Tracker Issue** \u2014 an issue in the team's tracker (GitHub, Jira, \u2026) that tracks a business intent or a piece of work.\n- **Domain** \u2014 a big area of the product (exists today in `product-os.yaml`). Its page is light (directional) or full (detailed); it holds no ICE; its spine `intents` names the business intents that touch it.\n- **Capability** \u2014 something the product can do, inside a domain (exists today in `product-os.yaml`). Its page is directional or detailed, and carries its ICE in the `## Agent intent (ICE)` section. It may sit in the holding folder `capabilities/<id>/` until its domain is known.\n- **Functionality** \u2014 one specific action inside a capability (the leaf of `product-os.yaml`). Its page carries its ICE in the `## Agent intent (ICE)` section.\n\n## Log\n\n- 2026-10-08 \u2014 First version, built through `build-product-ontology` for #612: six questions; kinds Business Intent, Source and ICE (reused from `ice.yaml`); Work, Tracker Issue, Domain, Capability and Function named but not yet defined. Working notes: `.garura/project/issues/612/specs/ontology-run.md`.\n- 2026-10-08 \u2014 v2: a Source is anything that explains the intent \u2014 a prototype, a document, or a plain statement \u2014 not only a prototype (#612, approving `/intent`'s intent). Snapshot and Kind widened to match.\n- 2026-10-08 \u2014 A Source may be a project folder (its written docs are kept; the running app is captured as a second, site Source).\n- 2026-10-09 \u2014 v3 (#612): no play owns a kind \u2014 any play writes what it finds, in any order; rules say what holds once aligned and never block a write; drift goes to the Alignment drive. Only a person confirms or drops a business intent. The writer rule for ICE is gone. (Where ICE lives and when it is workable: see the next entry, which replaced a first idea of separate ICE files.)\n- 2026-10-09 \u2014 Review of #631: \"Function\" renamed \"Functionality\" to match the glossary and the spine; any play may write a kind only inside its own declared write scope (`direct-model-write.md`).\n- 2026-10-09 \u2014 On review of #631: ICE lives inline in its node's grounding doc (spine v2), never as a separate file; a capability may exist before its domain (`/vision` attaches it); an ICE is workable only when built from a confirmed business intent (spine `intents`) \u2014 unlinked ICE is kept, not worked on, never blocked.\n- 2026-10-17 \u2014 v4 (#637): one product model that every play writes, at the depth it knows, and nothing waits for another play. ICE now has one named place \u2014 `## Agent intent (ICE)`, the last section of a capability's or functionality's page, in labelled lines filled as far as is known; expected once the node names a business intent (missing is a linter warning, so earlier pages still pass). A business intent's touches are kept on its page as a **Touches** list (adds / changes / removes, with links), and the domain's spine entry gains `intents`, so every part an intent touches is linked both ways. A domain page is light (directional) or full (detailed), like a capability's. Design: `.garura/project/issues/637/specs/design.md`.\n- 2026-10-18 \u2014 #656: personal names taken out of the Source example and of this Log, so the ontology can live in Phoenix memory for any project. No kind, field or rule changed."}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-003", "issue": 1, "play": "vision", "question": "Which domain does the goal 'shop owners lose fewer sales to abandoned carts' belong to?", "answer": "Ecommerce (medium confidence), with notifications and analytics as related domains.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:32:58Z", "trace": [{"asked": "which domain's trigger covers carts and orders", "found": "ecommerce triggers when the product sells goods \u2014 catalog, cart, orders, storefront", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 5, "end": 5, "fingerprint": "sha256:6b5ec069a363310f568b9f09f8c68e1dd5b35b45df74d5879cd69dcfbcd0301d", "text": "  trigger: \"the product sells physical or digital goods \u2014 catalog, cart, orders, storefront\"", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 2, "end": 8, "text": "id: domains/ecommerce\ntitle: \"Ecommerce: how we build it \u2014 content-first MACH, selected on the commerce\u2194content spectrum\"\nconditions:\n  trigger: \"the product sells physical or digital goods \u2014 catalog, cart, orders, storefront\"\n  selection_keys: [commerce-vs-content-weight, sku-count, traffic-volume, merchandising-depth, nfr.performance]\nprovenance: interview (#434) + an eCommerce selection-criterion reference\n---"}}}, {"asked": "which shopper intent an abandoned cart breaks", "found": "Acquire \u2014 let me buy it, cart, checkout handoff", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 34, "end": 34, "fingerprint": "sha256:6a005cc38f5849c37f52a233f2c3f3f81e3eb757123db7c4a5a4128d702c1b3b", "text": "- **Acquire** \u2014 \"let me buy it\" \u2192 cart, checkout handoff.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 31, "end": 37, "text": "**Shopper intents**\n- **Discover** \u2014 \"help me find what I want\" \u2192 browse, category, PLP, search.\n- **Evaluate** \u2014 \"help me decide\" \u2192 PDP, reviews & ratings, recommendations, compare.\n- **Acquire** \u2014 \"let me buy it\" \u2192 cart, checkout handoff.\n- **Track & manage** \u2014 \"where is it / change or return it\" \u2192 order status, returns, reorder.\n- **Be assisted** \u2014 \"shop with help\" \u2192 agentic / conversational commerce.\n"}}}, {"asked": "whether the reminder is a notifications concern", "found": "notifications triggers on reaching a user with a timely lifecycle message", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/notifications.md", "start": 5, "end": 5, "fingerprint": "sha256:26b681969a5df7415474a56cfa79141334afd5021ffc82aa575e6ce6e018ad93", "text": "  trigger: \"the product reaches a user with a timely message \u2014 in-app, push, email, SMS \u2014 transactional or lifecycle\"", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 2, "end": 8, "text": "id: domains/notifications\ntitle: \"Notifications: real-time, multi-channel, nudge-first \u2014 adapt the experience, not just ring a bell\"\nconditions:\n  trigger: \"the product reaches a user with a timely message \u2014 in-app, push, email, SMS \u2014 transactional or lifecycle\"\n  selection_keys: [presence-real-time, channel-mix, message-type, player-type-tailoring, consent-regime]\nprovenance: interview (#434)\n---"}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-004", "issue": 1, "play": "vision", "question": "Does the ecommerce shelf have a capability for recovering abandoned carts?", "answer": "No. Only an intelligence-feature line names cart-abandonment nudges; a new capability 'Abandoned-cart recovery' is proposed (memory update mu-20261020-001, waiting, not blocking).", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:32:59Z", "trace": [{"asked": "a capability for abandoned carts on the ecommerce shelf", "found": "only 'Acquire \u2014 cart-abandonment prediction with nudges; next-best-offer and personalised promotions' under intelligence features", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 150, "end": 151, "fingerprint": "sha256:71a0569b22ebf3fffc2ce33c9eb2f5a2e480e4fcaf09b881089d808fe9c4ce76", "text": "- *Acquire* \u2014 cart-abandonment prediction with nudges; next-best-offer and\n  personalised promotions.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 147, "end": 154, "text": "  shoppers query and question the reviews, and surface what other buyers actually\n  care about (review Q&A)**; size & fit recommendation; virtual try-on / AR;\n  auto-generated product comparisons.\n- *Acquire* \u2014 cart-abandonment prediction with nudges; next-best-offer and\n  personalised promotions.\n- *Be assisted* \u2014 **agentic / conversational commerce**; post-purchase support agent.\n- *Content production* \u2014 **brand-tone generation & checking** \u2014 produce and verify\n  on-brand copy that leads the glass. Core content work."}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-005", "issue": 1, "play": "vision", "question": "Which capabilities does the ecommerce shelf offer for this goal?", "answer": "Six candidates: seeing today's abandoned carts (same as abandoned-carts-today), reminding customers (same as abandoned-cart-reminders), tracking carts won back, carts and orders, abandonment prediction and in-session nudges, win-back offers.", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:32:59Z", "trace": [{"asked": "the record that tells an abandoned cart from a finished one", "found": "Inventory & order management Level 1 \u2014 order records, the floor for every store that sells", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 125, "end": 127, "fingerprint": "sha256:e02d1822f9e9902bc4aec262f260122b9f486c6368d06c611477a8fde7d0dc61", "text": "- **Inventory & order management** \u2014 core commerce; real-time stock when traffic\n  and reliability push (quadrants 3\u20134).\n  - Level 1 \u2014 basic stock & orders: simple counts and order records. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 122, "end": 130, "text": "  - Ratings & written reviews \u2014 collect and display buyer feedback. The floor when shoppers evaluate before buying.\n  - Moderation & verified-purchase \u2014 gate UGC and mark genuine buyers. Add when `shape.users: public` invites abuse, or `compliance` needs records.\n  - Review intelligence \u2014 AI summaries and consumer review Q&A on the PDP (see intelligence). Add for public, conversion-focused storefronts.\n- **Inventory & order management** \u2014 core commerce; real-time stock when traffic\n  and reliability push (quadrants 3\u20134).\n  - Level 1 \u2014 basic stock & orders: simple counts and order records. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.\n  - Level 2 \u2014 real-time stock & reservations: live availability under load. Add when `nfr.reliability >= high` and `nfr.performance >= high` (quadrants 3\u20134).\n  - Level 3 \u2014 distributed / omnichannel fulfilment: multi-warehouse, BOPIS, returns. Add when `shape.surfaces` spans channels or `nfr.scalability >= xhigh`.\n- **Integration / SOA layer** \u2014 heavy for quadrant 4: many back-end systems,"}}}, {"asked": "the core journey the cart sits in", "found": "home -> category -> PLP -> PDP -> cart -> checkout handoff", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 83, "end": 88, "fingerprint": "sha256:4d98b8fe8fbfe1f02ad1d95ec832417d9defa8e2eb4da37af1926812489c70c9", "text": "## The core journey \u2014 always there, or it breaks\nStart with the basics and make sure the core is always present. Without it the\njourney breaks, no matter the quadrant:\n\n**home \u2192 category pages \u2192 PLP (product listing) \u2192 PDP (product detail) \u2192 cart \u2192\ncheckout handoff.**", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 80, "end": 91, "text": "not where we lead. Keep them on the spectrum so the choice is honest; just know\nwhich engagements are ours to drive and which we route elsewhere.\n\n## The core journey \u2014 always there, or it breaks\nStart with the basics and make sure the core is always present. Without it the\njourney breaks, no matter the quadrant:\n\n**home \u2192 category pages \u2192 PLP (product listing) \u2192 PDP (product detail) \u2192 cart \u2192\ncheckout handoff.**\n\nBuild this spine first, end to end, before any enrichment. Everything else hangs\noff it."}}}, {"asked": "intelligence on cart abandonment", "found": "cart-abandonment prediction with nudges; next-best-offer and personalised promotions", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 150, "end": 151, "fingerprint": "sha256:71a0569b22ebf3fffc2ce33c9eb2f5a2e480e4fcaf09b881089d808fe9c4ce76", "text": "- *Acquire* \u2014 cart-abandonment prediction with nudges; next-best-offer and\n  personalised promotions.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 147, "end": 154, "text": "  shoppers query and question the reviews, and surface what other buyers actually\n  care about (review Q&A)**; size & fit recommendation; virtual try-on / AR;\n  auto-generated product comparisons.\n- *Acquire* \u2014 cart-abandonment prediction with nudges; next-best-offer and\n  personalised promotions.\n- *Be assisted* \u2014 **agentic / conversational commerce**; post-purchase support agent.\n- *Content production* \u2014 **brand-tone generation & checking** \u2014 produce and verify\n  on-brand copy that leads the glass. Core content work."}}}, {"asked": "how a reminder reaches an away shopper and under what consent", "found": "Email for away-from-app reach; opt-in/opt-out and fatigue control", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/notifications.md", "start": 70, "end": 74, "fingerprint": "sha256:f01076e9c9917deaba623bb647ac0d2a242fd3d53ab10e92682fa074dc39fa77", "text": "- **Channel delivery** \u2014 reach the user wherever they are. Multi-channel is the\n  floor, not a stretch (flat list \u2014 channels are independent, add per surface).\n  - In-app \u2014 the floor; messages and badges inside the product (`shape.surfaces` includes web/mobile/desktop).\n  - Push \u2014 when `shape.surfaces` includes mobile (or web with push support) and timely re-engagement matters.\n  - Email \u2014 when `shape.surfaces` includes web/api and you need durable, away-from-app reach.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 67, "end": 77, "text": "floor; add only when a profile dimension calls for it. These serve the POV above \u2014\nthey do not replace it.\n\n- **Channel delivery** \u2014 reach the user wherever they are. Multi-channel is the\n  floor, not a stretch (flat list \u2014 channels are independent, add per surface).\n  - In-app \u2014 the floor; messages and badges inside the product (`shape.surfaces` includes web/mobile/desktop).\n  - Push \u2014 when `shape.surfaces` includes mobile (or web with push support) and timely re-engagement matters.\n  - Email \u2014 when `shape.surfaces` includes web/api and you need durable, away-from-app reach.\n  - SMS \u2014 when `shape.surfaces` includes mobile and reach must not depend on app/email; add `nfr.privacy >= high` care for phone numbers.\n- **Templating** \u2014 one message, channel-appropriate renderings, authored via the experience layer.\n  - Per-channel templates \u2014 the floor once more than one channel exists; one message, channel-specific rendering composed where the experience is."}}}, {"asked": "how to measure carts won back", "found": "product-conversion funnel with drop-off at each step", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/analytics.md", "start": 63, "end": 64, "fingerprint": "sha256:797d140ca9c74c10b805d9f92339bca1415a6dd1eca16599ed89b3de3709211c", "text": "- **Funnels** \u2014 product/conversion funnels with drop-off, not discovery funnels.\n  - Product-conversion funnel \u2014 the in-product path to a goal (signup \u2192 activate \u2192 convert \u2192 retain) with drop-off at each step. The floor for this shelf.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 60, "end": 67, "text": "- **Goals** \u2014 explicit success definitions, the spine of value.\n  - Goal definition \u2014 name the success outcome and its event. The floor \u2014 without it you measure motion, not value.\n  - Goal hierarchy \u2014 primary vs assist goals, weighted. Add when one surface drives several outcomes worth ranking.\n- **Funnels** \u2014 product/conversion funnels with drop-off, not discovery funnels.\n  - Product-conversion funnel \u2014 the in-product path to a goal (signup \u2192 activate \u2192 convert \u2192 retain) with drop-off at each step. The floor for this shelf.\n  - Segmented funnel \u2014 same funnel split by cohort or property to locate where which users fall out. Add when one funnel hides divergent behaviour.\n- **Product analytics** \u2014 feature usage, adoption, retention, cohorts, paths.\n  - Feature usage & adoption \u2014 who uses which feature and how often. The floor for measuring the product, not just the site."}}}, {"asked": "what the owner asked for", "found": "see which carts were abandoned today and send a reminder with one click", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "1/context/intent/20261020-095249/statement.txt", "start": 1, "end": 1, "fingerprint": "sha256:4ccfe40b650f40dbaa7b75283b6dcaf64a603a9c3177e8d13b23f741832eaf07", "text": "Our shop owners lose sales when a customer abandons a cart. I want each owner to see which carts were abandoned today and send the customer a reminder with one click, so more carts turn into orders.", "version": null, "around": {"start": 1, "end": 1, "text": "Our shop owners lose sales when a customer abandons a cart. I want each owner to see which carts were abandoned today and send the customer a reminder with one click, so more carts turn into orders."}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-006", "issue": 1, "play": "vision", "question": "Which domain does the abandoned-cart work sit in?", "answer": "ecommerce, written as a detailed domain page", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:37:23Z", "trace": [{"asked": "which shelf's trigger names cart and orders, and where cart abandonment is named", "found": "ecommerce is the domain for carts and orders; cart abandonment sits under its Acquire intelligence", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 150, "end": 151, "fingerprint": "sha256:71a0569b22ebf3fffc2ce33c9eb2f5a2e480e4fcaf09b881089d808fe9c4ce76", "text": "- *Acquire* \u2014 cart-abandonment prediction with nudges; next-best-offer and\n  personalised promotions.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 147, "end": 154, "text": "  shoppers query and question the reviews, and surface what other buyers actually\n  care about (review Q&A)**; size & fit recommendation; virtual try-on / AR;\n  auto-generated product comparisons.\n- *Acquire* \u2014 cart-abandonment prediction with nudges; next-best-offer and\n  personalised promotions.\n- *Be assisted* \u2014 **agentic / conversational commerce**; post-purchase support agent.\n- *Content production* \u2014 **brand-tone generation & checking** \u2014 produce and verify\n  on-brand copy that leads the glass. Core content work."}}}, {"asked": "what records an abandoned cart is found from", "found": "basic stock and order records are the floor for every store that sells", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 125, "end": 127, "fingerprint": "sha256:e02d1822f9e9902bc4aec262f260122b9f486c6368d06c611477a8fde7d0dc61", "text": "- **Inventory & order management** \u2014 core commerce; real-time stock when traffic\n  and reliability push (quadrants 3\u20134).\n  - Level 1 \u2014 basic stock & orders: simple counts and order records. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 122, "end": 130, "text": "  - Ratings & written reviews \u2014 collect and display buyer feedback. The floor when shoppers evaluate before buying.\n  - Moderation & verified-purchase \u2014 gate UGC and mark genuine buyers. Add when `shape.users: public` invites abuse, or `compliance` needs records.\n  - Review intelligence \u2014 AI summaries and consumer review Q&A on the PDP (see intelligence). Add for public, conversion-focused storefronts.\n- **Inventory & order management** \u2014 core commerce; real-time stock when traffic\n  and reliability push (quadrants 3\u20134).\n  - Level 1 \u2014 basic stock & orders: simple counts and order records. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.\n  - Level 2 \u2014 real-time stock & reservations: live availability under load. Add when `nfr.reliability >= high` and `nfr.performance >= high` (quadrants 3\u20134).\n  - Level 3 \u2014 distributed / omnichannel fulfilment: multi-warehouse, BOPIS, returns. Add when `shape.surfaces` spans channels or `nfr.scalability >= xhigh`.\n- **Integration / SOA layer** \u2014 heavy for quadrant 4: many back-end systems,"}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-007", "issue": 1, "play": "vision", "question": "Is 'Tracking carts won back' grounded enough to add as a new capability?", "answer": "Yes \u2014 added as carts-won-back, proposed and directional", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:37:23Z", "trace": [{"asked": "a KB home for counting carts that end in an order", "found": "the product-conversion funnel with drop-off is the floor of the analytics shelf", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/analytics.md", "start": 63, "end": 64, "fingerprint": "sha256:797d140ca9c74c10b805d9f92339bca1415a6dd1eca16599ed89b3de3709211c", "text": "- **Funnels** \u2014 product/conversion funnels with drop-off, not discovery funnels.\n  - Product-conversion funnel \u2014 the in-product path to a goal (signup \u2192 activate \u2192 convert \u2192 retain) with drop-off at each step. The floor for this shelf.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 60, "end": 67, "text": "- **Goals** \u2014 explicit success definitions, the spine of value.\n  - Goal definition \u2014 name the success outcome and its event. The floor \u2014 without it you measure motion, not value.\n  - Goal hierarchy \u2014 primary vs assist goals, weighted. Add when one surface drives several outcomes worth ranking.\n- **Funnels** \u2014 product/conversion funnels with drop-off, not discovery funnels.\n  - Product-conversion funnel \u2014 the in-product path to a goal (signup \u2192 activate \u2192 convert \u2192 retain) with drop-off at each step. The floor for this shelf.\n  - Segmented funnel \u2014 same funnel split by cohort or property to locate where which users fall out. Add when one funnel hides divergent behaviour.\n- **Product analytics** \u2014 feature usage, adoption, retention, cohorts, paths.\n  - Feature usage & adoption \u2014 who uses which feature and how often. The floor for measuring the product, not just the site."}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-008", "issue": 1, "play": "vision", "question": "What limit does the reminder capability carry?", "answer": "A reminder goes only to a customer who agreed to hear from the shop, and never so often it becomes spam", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:37:23Z", "trace": [{"asked": "consent and frequency rules for any send", "found": "opt-in/opt-out is the floor; fatigue control caps low-value sends", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/notifications.md", "start": 83, "end": 86, "fingerprint": "sha256:a5922243ecc1a8df3a44b3e4ae04245bd64f9d0db59ac8b88abdebea7c789c42", "text": "- **Preferences & frequency** \u2014 let the user stay in control; never spam.\n  - Opt-in / opt-out \u2014 the floor; consent to be reached at all (`nfr.privacy >= medium`, or `compliance` includes GDPR).\n  - Per-channel preference \u2014 when more than one channel exists, so the user picks where each message type lands.\n  - Fatigue / frequency control \u2014 when message volume rises, or `shape.stage` is public/monetized; cap low-value sends to protect attention.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 80, "end": 89, "text": "  - Level 1 \u2014 presence detection: is the person in-session right now (the floor for routing at all).\n  - Level 2 \u2014 in-session experience-nudge vs channel-bell: present \u2192 hand to `experience`'s nudge layer; away \u2192 fire the channel. Add when real-time presence signals are reliable.\n  - Level 3 \u2014 player-type tailoring: shape the nudge to gamified behaviour (progress/achievement/exploration/social). Add when `personalization` supplies a behavioural profile.\n- **Preferences & frequency** \u2014 let the user stay in control; never spam.\n  - Opt-in / opt-out \u2014 the floor; consent to be reached at all (`nfr.privacy >= medium`, or `compliance` includes GDPR).\n  - Per-channel preference \u2014 when more than one channel exists, so the user picks where each message type lands.\n  - Fatigue / frequency control \u2014 when message volume rises, or `shape.stage` is public/monetized; cap low-value sends to protect attention.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n- **Fatigue & noise** \u2014 too many low-value messages; no frequency discipline."}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-009", "issue": 1, "play": "vision", "question": "Which guiding rules hold across the ecommerce domain?", "answer": "Core buying path stays whole; build on the records the shop already keeps", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:37:24Z", "trace": [{"asked": "the core journey", "found": "home to checkout handoff must always be present or the journey breaks", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 83, "end": 90, "fingerprint": "sha256:70a883aa511bc3b33544603225c853de11de95bbfa4dffee78fcc3bded323bd4", "text": "## The core journey \u2014 always there, or it breaks\nStart with the basics and make sure the core is always present. Without it the\njourney breaks, no matter the quadrant:\n\n**home \u2192 category pages \u2192 PLP (product listing) \u2192 PDP (product detail) \u2192 cart \u2192\ncheckout handoff.**\n\nBuild this spine first, end to end, before any enrichment. Everything else hangs", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 80, "end": 93, "text": "not where we lead. Keep them on the spectrum so the choice is honest; just know\nwhich engagements are ours to drive and which we route elsewhere.\n\n## The core journey \u2014 always there, or it breaks\nStart with the basics and make sure the core is always present. Without it the\njourney breaks, no matter the quadrant:\n\n**home \u2192 category pages \u2192 PLP (product listing) \u2192 PDP (product detail) \u2192 cart \u2192\ncheckout handoff.**\n\nBuild this spine first, end to end, before any enrichment. Everything else hangs\noff it.\n\n## Capabilities (after the spine, selected by quadrant)"}}}, {"asked": "reuse versus rebuild", "found": "don't rebuild what we've built", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 174, "end": 175, "fingerprint": "sha256:f9e61d18dfe18685664c75d2df846d4f3e28ce6675087add93282527ab3e3721", "text": "- **Don't rebuild what we've built** \u2014 start from the reference architecture and\n  reusable building blocks, not a blank page.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 171, "end": 178, "text": "  quadrants 3\u20134.\n- **API contracts are owned and stable** \u2014 the seams between MACH components are\n  the thing that doesn't change; define and govern them.\n- **Don't rebuild what we've built** \u2014 start from the reference architecture and\n  reusable building blocks, not a blank page.\n- **Don't treat all commerce the same** \u2014 pick the quadrant first; model variants\n  and similar SKU concerns separately per project.\n"}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-010", "issue": 1, "play": "vision", "question": "What directional privacy level does the profile take?", "answer": "medium", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:37:24Z", "trace": [{"asked": "what triggers consent handling for messages", "found": "opt-in is required where nfr.privacy >= medium", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/notifications.md", "start": 83, "end": 86, "fingerprint": "sha256:a5922243ecc1a8df3a44b3e4ae04245bd64f9d0db59ac8b88abdebea7c789c42", "text": "- **Preferences & frequency** \u2014 let the user stay in control; never spam.\n  - Opt-in / opt-out \u2014 the floor; consent to be reached at all (`nfr.privacy >= medium`, or `compliance` includes GDPR).\n  - Per-channel preference \u2014 when more than one channel exists, so the user picks where each message type lands.\n  - Fatigue / frequency control \u2014 when message volume rises, or `shape.stage` is public/monetized; cap low-value sends to protect attention.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 80, "end": 89, "text": "  - Level 1 \u2014 presence detection: is the person in-session right now (the floor for routing at all).\n  - Level 2 \u2014 in-session experience-nudge vs channel-bell: present \u2192 hand to `experience`'s nudge layer; away \u2192 fire the channel. Add when real-time presence signals are reliable.\n  - Level 3 \u2014 player-type tailoring: shape the nudge to gamified behaviour (progress/achievement/exploration/social). Add when `personalization` supplies a behavioural profile.\n- **Preferences & frequency** \u2014 let the user stay in control; never spam.\n  - Opt-in / opt-out \u2014 the floor; consent to be reached at all (`nfr.privacy >= medium`, or `compliance` includes GDPR).\n  - Per-channel preference \u2014 when more than one channel exists, so the user picks where each message type lands.\n  - Fatigue / frequency control \u2014 when message volume rises, or `shape.stage` is public/monetized; cap low-value sends to protect attention.\n\n## Where it goes wrong\n- **Fatigue & noise** \u2014 too many low-value messages; no frequency discipline."}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-011", "issue": 1, "play": "understand", "question": "Where in the knowledge base does 'Seeing today's abandoned carts' belong?", "answer": "ecommerce -> Abandoned-cart recovery (the approved shelf addition), resting on Inventory & order management Level 1", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:55:45Z", "trace": [{"asked": "which domain's trigger covers carts and orders", "found": "ecommerce is the only shelf whose trigger names cart and orders", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 5, "end": 5, "fingerprint": "sha256:6b5ec069a363310f568b9f09f8c68e1dd5b35b45df74d5879cd69dcfbcd0301d", "text": "  trigger: \"the product sells physical or digital goods \u2014 catalog, cart, orders, storefront\"", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 2, "end": 8, "text": "id: domains/ecommerce\ntitle: \"Ecommerce: how we build it \u2014 content-first MACH, selected on the commerce\u2194content spectrum\"\nconditions:\n  trigger: \"the product sells physical or digital goods \u2014 catalog, cart, orders, storefront\"\n  selection_keys: [commerce-vs-content-weight, sku-count, traffic-volume, merchandising-depth, nfr.performance]\nprovenance: interview (#434) + an eCommerce selection-criterion reference\n---"}}}, {"asked": "a capability on the shelf that owns seeing abandoned carts", "found": "the approved addition Abandoned-cart recovery makes those carts visible to the operator", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "1/context/answers/kb/ecommerce-abandoned-cart-recovery.md", "start": 1, "end": 8, "fingerprint": "sha256:13e9593ced352e2eb52627ae3520ac07810ece177b8fd5fb5319d0dfaae7728d", "text": "- **Abandoned-cart recovery** \u2014 win back the sales a shopper started and left.\n  A cart that holds items but ends with no order is a lost sale the operator can\n  still recover; the capability makes those carts visible to the operator and\n  brings the shopper back to finish. It serves the shopper's *Acquire* intent\n  (\"let me buy it\") when it broke off, and the operator's *Understand* intent.\n  Sending the message is `notifications` (channel delivery, consent, frequency);\n  measuring the win-back is `analytics` (conversion funnel); this capability owns\n  the decision of WHICH cart, WHEN, and the way back into the same cart.", "version": null, "around": {"start": 1, "end": 11, "text": "- **Abandoned-cart recovery** \u2014 win back the sales a shopper started and left.\n  A cart that holds items but ends with no order is a lost sale the operator can\n  still recover; the capability makes those carts visible to the operator and\n  brings the shopper back to finish. It serves the shopper's *Acquire* intent\n  (\"let me buy it\") when it broke off, and the operator's *Understand* intent.\n  Sending the message is `notifications` (channel delivery, consent, frequency);\n  measuring the win-back is `analytics` (conversion funnel); this capability owns\n  the decision of WHICH cart, WHEN, and the way back into the same cart.\n  - Abandoned-cart detection \u2014 mark a cart abandoned when it holds items and no order follows within a set window. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.\n  - Operator view of abandoned carts \u2014 list the carts abandoned in a period (e.g. today) with shopper, items and value, so the operator knows which sales could still be won back. The floor wherever an operator runs the store.\n  - Cart reminder \u2014 operator-triggered (one click) or automatic message to the shopper that their cart is waiting, with a link that restores the same cart. Add when the shopper can be reached away from the store (`notifications` \u2192 Channel delivery); requires opt-in where `nfr.privacy >= medium` or `compliance` includes GDPR."}}}, {"asked": "the records the view is built on", "found": "Inventory & order management Level 1 keeps basic stock and order records", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 125, "end": 127, "fingerprint": "sha256:e02d1822f9e9902bc4aec262f260122b9f486c6368d06c611477a8fde7d0dc61", "text": "- **Inventory & order management** \u2014 core commerce; real-time stock when traffic\n  and reliability push (quadrants 3\u20134).\n  - Level 1 \u2014 basic stock & orders: simple counts and order records. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 122, "end": 130, "text": "  - Ratings & written reviews \u2014 collect and display buyer feedback. The floor when shoppers evaluate before buying.\n  - Moderation & verified-purchase \u2014 gate UGC and mark genuine buyers. Add when `shape.users: public` invites abuse, or `compliance` needs records.\n  - Review intelligence \u2014 AI summaries and consumer review Q&A on the PDP (see intelligence). Add for public, conversion-focused storefronts.\n- **Inventory & order management** \u2014 core commerce; real-time stock when traffic\n  and reliability push (quadrants 3\u20134).\n  - Level 1 \u2014 basic stock & orders: simple counts and order records. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.\n  - Level 2 \u2014 real-time stock & reservations: live availability under load. Add when `nfr.reliability >= high` and `nfr.performance >= high` (quadrants 3\u20134).\n  - Level 3 \u2014 distributed / omnichannel fulfilment: multi-warehouse, BOPIS, returns. Add when `shape.surfaces` spans channels or `nfr.scalability >= xhigh`.\n- **Integration / SOA layer** \u2014 heavy for quadrant 4: many back-end systems,"}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-012", "issue": 1, "play": "understand", "question": "Which functionalities does the capability break into?", "answer": "Abandoned-cart detection and Today's abandoned-carts list", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:55:45Z", "trace": [{"asked": "how a cart becomes abandoned", "found": "mark a cart abandoned when it holds items and no order follows within a set window", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "1/context/answers/kb/ecommerce-abandoned-cart-recovery.md", "start": 9, "end": 9, "fingerprint": "sha256:03dd04430213715ba73034e24b03b316f0a19c8828734695c55160a98a78a811", "text": "  - Abandoned-cart detection \u2014 mark a cart abandoned when it holds items and no order follows within a set window. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.", "version": null, "around": {"start": 6, "end": 12, "text": "  Sending the message is `notifications` (channel delivery, consent, frequency);\n  measuring the win-back is `analytics` (conversion funnel); this capability owns\n  the decision of WHICH cart, WHEN, and the way back into the same cart.\n  - Abandoned-cart detection \u2014 mark a cart abandoned when it holds items and no order follows within a set window. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.\n  - Operator view of abandoned carts \u2014 list the carts abandoned in a period (e.g. today) with shopper, items and value, so the operator knows which sales could still be won back. The floor wherever an operator runs the store.\n  - Cart reminder \u2014 operator-triggered (one click) or automatic message to the shopper that their cart is waiting, with a link that restores the same cart. Add when the shopper can be reached away from the store (`notifications` \u2192 Channel delivery); requires opt-in where `nfr.privacy >= medium` or `compliance` includes GDPR.\n  - Recovery tracking \u2014 count reminded carts that end in an order and the sales won back, over time. Add when the business sets a win-back goal (`analytics` \u2192 Goals, Funnels)."}}}, {"asked": "what the owner sees", "found": "list the carts abandoned in a period such as today with shopper, items and value", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "1/context/answers/kb/ecommerce-abandoned-cart-recovery.md", "start": 10, "end": 10, "fingerprint": "sha256:56fc764db48d724eb8f1147f0894678b458f3ee6c184d5d638859190e4a53551", "text": "  - Operator view of abandoned carts \u2014 list the carts abandoned in a period (e.g. today) with shopper, items and value, so the operator knows which sales could still be won back. The floor wherever an operator runs the store.", "version": null, "around": {"start": 7, "end": 13, "text": "  measuring the win-back is `analytics` (conversion funnel); this capability owns\n  the decision of WHICH cart, WHEN, and the way back into the same cart.\n  - Abandoned-cart detection \u2014 mark a cart abandoned when it holds items and no order follows within a set window. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.\n  - Operator view of abandoned carts \u2014 list the carts abandoned in a period (e.g. today) with shopper, items and value, so the operator knows which sales could still be won back. The floor wherever an operator runs the store.\n  - Cart reminder \u2014 operator-triggered (one click) or automatic message to the shopper that their cart is waiting, with a link that restores the same cart. Add when the shopper can be reached away from the store (`notifications` \u2192 Channel delivery); requires opt-in where `nfr.privacy >= medium` or `compliance` includes GDPR.\n  - Recovery tracking \u2014 count reminded carts that end in an order and the sales won back, over time. Add when the business sets a win-back goal (`analytics` \u2192 Goals, Funnels).\n  - Reminder sequences & incentives \u2014 timed follow-ups and an optional offer (next-best-offer / personalised promotion). Add when `shape.stage` is public/monetized and fatigue control is in place."}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-013", "issue": 1, "play": "understand", "question": "Where are the capability's edges with its neighbours?", "answer": "It shows carts only; reminding is abandoned-cart-reminders and counting win-backs is carts-won-back", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:55:45Z", "trace": [{"asked": "who sends the message and who measures the win-back", "found": "sending is notifications, measuring is analytics; the recovery capability owns which cart and when", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "1/context/answers/kb/ecommerce-abandoned-cart-recovery.md", "start": 1, "end": 8, "fingerprint": "sha256:13e9593ced352e2eb52627ae3520ac07810ece177b8fd5fb5319d0dfaae7728d", "text": "- **Abandoned-cart recovery** \u2014 win back the sales a shopper started and left.\n  A cart that holds items but ends with no order is a lost sale the operator can\n  still recover; the capability makes those carts visible to the operator and\n  brings the shopper back to finish. It serves the shopper's *Acquire* intent\n  (\"let me buy it\") when it broke off, and the operator's *Understand* intent.\n  Sending the message is `notifications` (channel delivery, consent, frequency);\n  measuring the win-back is `analytics` (conversion funnel); this capability owns\n  the decision of WHICH cart, WHEN, and the way back into the same cart.", "version": null, "around": {"start": 1, "end": 11, "text": "- **Abandoned-cart recovery** \u2014 win back the sales a shopper started and left.\n  A cart that holds items but ends with no order is a lost sale the operator can\n  still recover; the capability makes those carts visible to the operator and\n  brings the shopper back to finish. It serves the shopper's *Acquire* intent\n  (\"let me buy it\") when it broke off, and the operator's *Understand* intent.\n  Sending the message is `notifications` (channel delivery, consent, frequency);\n  measuring the win-back is `analytics` (conversion funnel); this capability owns\n  the decision of WHICH cart, WHEN, and the way back into the same cart.\n  - Abandoned-cart detection \u2014 mark a cart abandoned when it holds items and no order follows within a set window. The floor \u2014 every store that sells.\n  - Operator view of abandoned carts \u2014 list the carts abandoned in a period (e.g. today) with shopper, items and value, so the operator knows which sales could still be won back. The floor wherever an operator runs the store.\n  - Cart reminder \u2014 operator-triggered (one click) or automatic message to the shopper that their cart is waiting, with a link that restores the same cart. Add when the shopper can be reached away from the store (`notifications` \u2192 Channel delivery); requires opt-in where `nfr.privacy >= medium` or `compliance` includes GDPR."}}}, {"asked": "which owner intent the view serves", "found": "the operator's Understand intent, learning from behaviour", "source": {"tier": "phoenix", "path": "knowledge/domains/ecommerce.md", "start": 38, "end": 41, "fingerprint": "sha256:edaf4ef3cddcea0a06c94bac8b38293faf3c0d3277d31db9645d2c972c5c22e2", "text": "**Operator (business) intents**\n- **Merchandise** \u2014 \"present and prioritise the catalog\" \u2192 merchandising, PLP curation.\n- **Enrich** \u2014 \"make products rich and on-brand\" \u2192 product content, brand-tone.\n- **Understand** \u2014 \"learn from behaviour and reviews\" \u2192 analytics, review analysis.", "version": "db6441a93356", "around": {"start": 35, "end": 44, "text": "- **Track & manage** \u2014 \"where is it / change or return it\" \u2192 order status, returns, reorder.\n- **Be assisted** \u2014 \"shop with help\" \u2192 agentic / conversational commerce.\n\n**Operator (business) intents**\n- **Merchandise** \u2014 \"present and prioritise the catalog\" \u2192 merchandising, PLP curation.\n- **Enrich** \u2014 \"make products rich and on-brand\" \u2192 product content, brand-tone.\n- **Understand** \u2014 \"learn from behaviour and reviews\" \u2192 analytics, review analysis.\n- **Operate** \u2014 \"keep stock and orders flowing\" \u2192 inventory, fulfilment.\n\n## Pick the shape first (the commerce\u2194content spectrum \u2014 our POV)"}}}], "from_update": null}, {"citation_id": "cite-20261020-014", "issue": 1, "play": "understand", "question": "How long must a cart sit untouched before it counts as abandoned?", "answer": "Left open: the docs say 'the abandonment window'; memory update mu-20261020-002 asks the owner for its length", "decided_by": "product-os-keeper", "decided_at": "2026-10-20T04:55:46Z", "trace": [{"asked": "a length for the abandonment window", "found": "memory says only 'within a set window' and names no length", "source": {"tier": "short-term", "path": "1/context/answers/kb/ecommerce-abandoned-cart-recovery.md", "start": 9, "end": 9, "fingerprint": "sha256:03dd04430213715ba73034e24b03b316f0a19c8828734695c55160a98a78a811", "text": "  - Abandoned-cart detection \u2014 mark a cart abandoned when it holds items and no order follows within a set window. Th